PATCH 481: the extender service wrappers e1d1 / e132 / e189 dropped e339's RETURN -- the last
native<->wasm divergence of the AZER1 self-play, present from the very first mission frame.

asm 0xe1d1 / 0xe132 / 0xe189: `mov gs:[si+0x3f2],ebx; mov [0xea10],<selector>; call e339; ret` -- the
service result is e339's AX and the callers consume it: adcd (patch 222) and 9db1 (patch 298) store it as
the object's ground height byte[obj+0xd], and 9e2b slews the hull toward it.  Ghidra emitted a bare
`return;` in a function declared to return undefined4; native -O0 happened to leave e339's AX in the
return register while wasm -O2 did not, so every object's byte[+0xd] held a real terrain height on wasm and
0 on native.  Found by adding a per-tick 32-block DGROUP fingerprint (FIST_SIMHASH) plus a raw region dump
(FIST_SIMDUMP) to the cross-target trace and diffing the two targets at the first in-mission tick: exactly
one byte per object, [obj+0x0d].

Shim (tools/native_main.c): the op-0x54 TERRAIN-HEIGHT service now answers OBJECT queries too, not just
the projectile one -- it takes the position pair the engine posts in the TCB inbox (TCB+0x3f2 = obj+4) and
returns the heightmap byte with the same base (ext+0x85bc) and index packing as the decoded op-0x58 LOS
handler.  Every object's ground height was 0 before, which is also why the op-0x58 note reports units that
do not sit on the terrain.
--- a/fist.c
+++ b/fist.c
@@ -36072,10 +36072,15 @@
 undefined4 __allregs FUN_0000_e132(undefined4 param_1)
 
 {
+  /* PATCH 481: asm `mov gs:[si+0x3f2],ebx; mov [0xea10],SEL; call e339; ret` -- the service RESULT is
+   * e339's AX and the callers use it (adcd/9db1 store it as the object's ground height byte[di+0xd],
+   * 9e2b slews to it).  Ghidra emitted a bare `return;` in a non-void function: native -O0 happened
+   * to leave e339's AX in place, wasm -O2 did not -- so EVERY object's byte[+0xd] was a real terrain
+   * height on wasm and 0 on native, the first (and until now only) native<->wasm divergence of the
+   * AZER1 self-play, visible from the very first mission frame. */
   *(undefined4 *)(DAT_2000_aa2c + 0x3f2) = param_1;
   DAT_2000_aa10 = 0x40;
-  FUN_0000_e339();
-  return;
+  return FUN_0000_e339();
 }
 
 
